<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<class name="GameModeBase" inherits="Node" api_type="extension" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/master/doc/class.xsd">
	<brief_description>
		The rules of a level, and the scenes everything else is built from.
	</brief_description>
	<description>
		It exists on the server only: it decides who may join, where they spawn and when the match is over. The list of players everyone is allowed to see lives on the [GameStateBase] instead.
		A game mode is a scene whose root is a [GameModeBase], pointed at by [member Level.game_mode_override] or by the [code]application/game_framework/default_game_mode[/code] project setting. Its default scenes - [member game_state_scene], [member player_state_scene], [member player_controller_scene], [member default_pawn_scene] - are read on a client too, without the game mode ever being part of that client's world: both sides have to build the same things for a player.
		Override [code]_init_game[/code] to set the level up (return [code]true[/code] to take spawning over completely), [code]_pre_login[/code] to refuse a connecting peer, [code]_on_post_login[/code] and [code]_on_logout[/code] to follow players in and out, and [code]_get_pawn_scene_for[/code] to choose a pawn per player.
	</description>
